the number of people hospitalized with  cova 19 has been a key statistic in  forming policy throughout the pandemic  but not all covet hospitalizations are  created equal as the omicron wave puts a  strain on healthcare systems across  canada some jurisdictions have started  separating hospitalization numbers based  on whether a patient was admitted for  cover 19 or for other reasons but tested  positive during screening the latter are  often known as incidental covet  hospitalizations that means not everyone  who is in hospital with covid was  admitted because of covid however the  country's top doctor says the key thing  to remember when setting public health  policy is that hospitals are still  stretched thin whether a patient has  incidental covid or not  i guess the bottom line is this variant  is having a very significant impact on  the hospitalizations but the other thing  that's very different is that of course  staff are getting sick at the same time  so that is putting a lot of pressure on  the hospital systems  the numbers vary from province to  province with about one-third of coveted  hospital admissions in saskatchewan  being considered incidental while in  ontario that number is closer to half  icu numbers also differ from province to  province for example ontario has 82  percent of cova 19 patients in the icu  admitted for covid as of january 18th  compared to 18 percent who are  considered incidental infectious  diseases specialist dr isaac bogash says  determining which cases are incidental  can be difficult  you have people who have a copin 19  infection who have overt signs and  symptoms of copa 19 that bring them into  hospital sometimes you have people who  have a cova 19 infection but it  exacerbates an underlying medical  condition so someone has an exacerbation  of their emphysema someone has an  abnormal heart rhythm someone has a fall  and a lot a lot of this was driven by a  copa 19 infection it's very  i'd be very curious to see how those  individuals are categorized some people  of course have something completely  unrelated to coven 19. totally unrelated  they come into hospital and have an  incidental finding of cope at 19. it's  important to know some people acquire  copa 19 in hospital as well it's also  important to know dr nathan stahl at  mount sinai hospital in toronto says the  number of incidental cova 19 patients  should be interpreted with care when i  look at it it is one piece of data that  i you know question you know the the  proportions and numbers that i'm seeing  based on the challenges with definitions  i  i do not consider it nearly as as  importantly as things like number of  hospitalizations and ic admissions and  staffing shortages and and the  downstream consequences to care also at  issue is the impact a covert positive  patient can have on the health care  system when a person tests positive  whether they were admitted for covid or  with an incidental case it can trigger  the need for more stringent health care  protocols to protect both staff and  other patients dr jesse pappenberg of  montreal children's hospital says that  in turn creates further strain  any person with a saris kobe 2 infection  depending on what type of procedures  done what what what clinical condition  the patient is in may require additional  infection control measures to be put  into place which are burdensome  and costly for the healthcare system  there's also been concern among some  health professionals about whether  releasing incidental data is helpful  stahl is concerned releasing these covet  hospitalization numbers may result in  the downplaying of the severity of the  virus  so if it helps health systems plan for  therapeutics and for anticipated  critical care use and and and that sort  of thing i think it could be helpful it  can be weaponized the data can be  weaponized in so many ways that  downplays a real  difficulty in human tragedy for so many  people across this country provincial  health officials have defended the  decision  it does have an impact on things like  infection control practices in the  hospital  but it does also give us a measure of  where we are in terms of of impact we're  providing this data to inform planning  and in alignment with other provinces  reporting  however it is important to recognize  that any covet covered positive  admission has an impact on our acute  care capacity  opinions are divided on how to interpret  these hospitalization numbers but  canada's deputy chief public health  officer dr howard news says regardless  of why they were admitted the focus  should still be on reducing the overall  burden on the health care system sean  previl global news  thanks for watching global news if  you're interested in more national and  international news be sure to subscribe  to our channel
 

